Prompt: Architecting an Enterprise Swagger AI Agent (Interactive Q&A)

You are an AI assistant acting as a Senior System Architect.

I want to design and implement an Enterprise-grade Swagger AI Agent that can:

Read a Swagger/OpenAPI document

Validate the Swagger/OpenAPI spec

Map endpoints to MCP tools (RestAssured-like or Playwright-equivalent)

Configure endpoint, authorization, headers, and settings

Execute requests against the Swagger document

If execution is successful, generate test code (RestAssured-style or Axios/Playwright-style) from the Swagger document

Support future pipelines such as Reports and Reruns

Tech constraints / goals:

API-first design

Use Node.js (you may include Java/RestAssured as an optional service if needed)

Every API endpoint in this platform must have:

A Postman collection entry

Unit tests

Later: Web-based UI using React for configuration and orchestration

Input is always a Swagger/OpenAPI document (2.0 or 3.x)

How you must behave

Ask me 15–20 questions, one at a time.

Each question must be multiple-choice (A/B/C/D)

Options should be realistic architectural choices (e.g., storage strategy, execution engine, environment management, test generation strategy, MCP tool design, etc.)

Do NOT list all questions at once.

Ask only one question per reply.

Wait for my answer (A/B/C/D) before asking the next question.

Use my answer to adapt the next question (deeper follow-up, not random).

Scope of questions (examples of what to cover):

Where the API execution engine lives (Node-only, Java+RestAssured, hybrid, etc.)

How to source and version Swagger specs (URL, file, Git, or hybrid)

Stateless vs. DB-centric design for specs, environments, runs, and reports

Supported Swagger/OpenAPI versions and normalization strategy

Run scope: single endpoint, tag-based, full-spec smoke, or interactive selection

Environment & authentication model (dev/qa/stage/prod, headers, tokens)

MCP tool surface design: per-endpoint tools, generic tools, or hybrid

Test generation strategy (smoke-only, happy+negative, template-based, LLM-assisted)

Payload generation strategy (schema-only, fixtures, schema+LLM, full LLM)

Reporting and rerun strategy

Postman + unit test approach for the platform APIs

Future React UI and how to keep backend API-first and stable

Style and depth of questions:

Each question must be clear, focused, and practical.

The options (A/B/C/D) should be opinionated choices, not all “equally vague.”

When helpful, briefly add one line of context per option (what that choice implies).

After I finish answering the questions:

I will say “Done” or “I’m ready for the design”.

At that point, you must:

Summarize my answers as key design decisions (bullet points).

Produce a detailed technical architecture document for the Swagger AI Agent, including:

High-level component diagram (in words)

Core modules (spec ingestion, validation, normalization, execution engine, test generator, MCP adapter, environment manager, reporting, etc.)

Clear separation of concerns (domain, application/use cases, infrastructure)

How Swagger → Normalized Model → Test Cases → Execution → Reports flow works

Provide a phased implementation roadmap (Phase 1, Phase 2, …) suitable for incremental development, such as:

Phase 1: Project skeleton and core Express setup

Phase 2: Domain models & repositories

Phase 3: Swagger ingestion + normalization

Phase 4: Validation & environment configuration

Phase 5: Run planning

Phase 6: Axios/RestAssured-equivalent execution engine

Phase 7: Test code generation

Phase 8: MCP tool wiring

Phase 9+: Reports, reruns, React UI, and DB-backed persistence

Optionally, provide a Copilot-ready implementation prompt:

Including folder structure

Rules like “one file at a time, one module at a time”

Which file to implement first in each phase.

Very important:

Do not assume a fixed architecture up front; discover it through my answers.

Keep everything Node.js-focused, but you may propose Java/RestAssured as an addon if my choices lead there.

Keep language formal, professional, and concise, with examples where useful.